e (i.e., all cards are sequential) ignoring gaps using rank columns (V2, V4, V6, V8, V10). Calculation involves sorting ranks, finding differences, and verifying all diffs equal 1 (or adjusted for Ace-low straights).\ndf[\'Is_Straight_Sequence\'] = df[[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\']].apply(lambda row: \n    sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), min(row)+5)) or \n    sorted(row) == [1, 10, 11, 12, 13], axis=1).astype(int)\n# Usefulness: This feature correlates directly with hands like "Straight" and "Straight Flush". Capturing sequence continuity directly helps the model identify hands relying on card order dependencies, enhancing predictive performance for sequential combinations.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Adding a feature to count suits appearing more than once\n```python\n# Feature name: Suit_Repeated_Count\n# Feature description: Counts how many suits appear more than once in the hand using suit attributes (V1, V3, V5, V7, V9).\ndf[\'Suit_Repeated_Count\'] = df[[\'V1\', \'V3\', \'V5\', \'V7\', \'V9\']].apply(lambda row: \n    sum([row.tolist().count(suit) for suit in set(row) if row.tolist().count(suit) > 1]), axis=1)\n# Usefulness: This feature indirectly captures partial "Flush"-like patterns where suits are distributed unevenly across the hand. It provides additional discriminatory power to differentiate suits and poker hands that exhibit suit dependencies.\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping a column where derivative features exist or information is minimal\nFor feature selection and simplification:\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Is_Same_Suit is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=[\'Is_Same_Suit\'], inplace=True)\n# Is_Same_Suit duplicates the information captured by Suit_Repeated_Count, which offers more granular insights into suit patterns by counting repetitions instead of a binary indicator. Thus, retaining Suit_Repeated_Count provides a better signal for Flush-like hands.\n```end']
