ssary (e.g., highly correlated with other features or provides no unique value), it can be dropped with explanation.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column V1 is dropped\n# Column V1 (Suit of card #1) may be dropped if the dataset includes a derived column like Is_Same_Suit that summarizes the information. Dropping this prevents redundancy while maintaining information.\ndf.drop(columns=['V1'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Reasoning for Proposed Features\n\n1. Is_Same_Suit: Helps capture Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush hands, which require all cards to share the same suit. Sparse presence of suit uniformity in generic poker hands makes this a critical signal for high-value outcomes.\n   \n2. Rank_Differences_Mean: Sequentiality is a key trait of Straight and Straight Flush hands. This feature quantifies it explicitly by analyzing rank-level differences within individual hands.\n\n3. Rank_Counts_Max: Helps explain hands with card rank repetitions (e.g., pairs, Full House, Four of a Kind). This numerical feature improves performance substantially on intermediate poker hand classifications.", "### Step 1: Adding a feature to count unique ranks in the hand\npython\n# Feature name: Unique_Rank_Count\n# Feature description: Counts the number of unique ranks in the hand using the rank attributes (V2, V4, V6, V8, V10).\ndf['Unique_Rank_Count'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].nunique(axis=1)\n# Usefulness: Poker hands (Two pair, Three of a kind, Four of a kind, Full house) rely on the counts of repeated ranks. The number of unique ranks can directly help identify pairs, triplets, quartets, and distinguish these hands from others. Highly relevant for influencing the target variable.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Adding a feature to check sequence continuity\npython\n# Feature name: Is_Straight_Sequence\n# Feature description: Checks if the ranks form a valid sequence (i.e., all cards are sequential) ignoring gaps using rank columns (V2, V4, V6, V8, V10). Calculation involves sorting ranks, finding differences, and verifying all diffs equal 1 (or adjusted for Ace-low straights).\ndf['Is_Straight_Sequence'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda row: \n    sorted(row) == list(range(min(row), min(row)+5)) or \n    sorted(row) == [1, 10, 11, 12, 13], axis=1).astype(int)\n# Usefulness: This feature correlates directly with hands like Straight and Straight Flush. Capturing sequence continuity directly helps the model identify hands relying on card order dependencies, enhancing predictive performance for sequential combinations.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Adding a feature to count suits appearing more than once\npython\n# Feature name: Suit_Repeated_Count\n# Feature description: Counts how many suits appear more than once in the hand using suit attributes (V1, V3, V5, V7, V9).\ndf['Suit_Repeated_Count'] = df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].apply(lambda row: \n    sum([row.tolist().count(suit) for suit in set(row) if row.tolist().count(suit) > 1]), axis=1)\n# Usefulness: This feature indirectly captures partial Flush-like patterns where suits are distributed unevenly across the hand. It provides additional discriminatory power to differentiate suits and poker hands that exhibit suit dependencies.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping a column where derivative features exist or information is minimal\nFor feature selection and simplification:\npython\n# Explanation why the column Is_Same_Suit is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Is_Same_Suit'], inplace=True)\n# Is_Same_Suit duplicates the information captured by Suit_Repeated_Count, which offers more granular insights into suit patterns by counting repetitions instead of a binary indicator. Thus, retaining Suit_Repeated_Count provides a better signal for Flush-like hands.\nend"), with improvements -
