 dropped\n# Rank_Mode_Frequency is less informative since we now have Suit_Mode_Frequency which offers more targeted information regarding flush-based hands, as well as Rank_Sequentiality and Rank_Diff_Max_Min which together provide richer insights into rank distributions for straights and other patterns. Removing redundancy improves model clarity.\ndf.drop(columns=['Rank_Mode_Frequency'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Feature name: Rank_Frequency_Distribution\n# Feature description: Apply `rank` operation on Rank_Mode_Frequency to categorize cards based on the commonness of their ranks. This creates a feature denoting the overall frequency distribution of ranks per hand.\n# Usefulness: Provides a categorical perspective on rank frequency, which could help identify patterns or combinations like pairs, three-of-a-kinds, full houses, etc., for better downstream task performance.\n\ndf['Rank_Frequency_Distribution'] = df['Rank_Mode_Frequency'].rank() \n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Sequential_Rank_Sum\n# Feature description: Apply the `cumsum` operation to generate a running sum across rank features (e.g., V2, V4, V6, V8, V10), followed by extracting the total.\n# Usefulness: Highlights whether the ranks in the hand form sequences, helping identify straights, straight flushes, and high-level poker hands with sequential ranks (especially for cases like Royal Flush). \n\ndf['Sequential_Rank_Sum'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda x: x.sum(), axis=1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: Suit_Consistency_Measure\n# Feature description: Apply the `ratio` operator on Total_Same_Suit_Cards divided by 5 (total cards in the hand), generating proportion of cards with the same suit.\n# Usefulness: Helps quantify hands more likely to form a flush, straight flush, or royal flush, and adds granularity to suit-based predictions.\n\ndf['Suit_Consistency_Measure'] = df['Total_Same_Suit_Cards'] / 5\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column Rank_